steps.body:
A field whose name starts with `#` is **private**: only code inside the class body can read or write it. Declare it in the class body, with a starting value (`#code = 1234;`) or bare (`#width;`) when the constructor fills it in, then use it through `this`. Using a `#name` the class never declared is also a `SyntaxError`. Methods can be private too.

```javascript
class Safe {
  #code = 1234;

  #matches(guess) {
    return guess === this.#code;
  }

  open(guess) {
    return this.#matches(guess) ? "open" : "locked";
  }
}
```

Why bother? A public field can be set to anything from anywhere, like `account.balance = -5` in the last lesson. A private one changes only through the public methods you write, so those methods can check every change.

Writing `safe.#code` outside the class is a `SyntaxError`. Node rejects the whole file before a single line runs, so the mistake never sneaks into a running program. Plain `safe.code` is a different name and just reads `undefined`.

Private fields also stay out of `JSON.stringify`, which lists only public fields. That makes it a handy way to see what an object exposes. Never `console.log` an instance to check this, because Node's inspect format is made for debugging, not for checked output.

example.code:
class Wallet {
  #balance = 0;

  constructor(owner) {
    this.owner = owner;
  }

  #canSpend(amount) {
    return amount > 0 && amount <= this.#balance;
  }

  deposit(amount) {
    if (amount > 0) {
      this.#balance += amount;
    }
  }

  spend(amount) {
    if (this.#canSpend(amount)) {
      this.#balance -= amount;
    }
  }

  getBalance() {
    return this.#balance;
  }
}

const wallet = new Wallet("Ada");
wallet.deposit(50);
console.log(`After deposit 50: ${wallet.getBalance()}`);
wallet.spend(80);
console.log(`After spend 80: ${wallet.getBalance()}`);
wallet.spend(30);
console.log(`After spend 30: ${wallet.getBalance()}`);
console.log(JSON.stringify(wallet));

