practice.solution:
class Counter {
  #count = 0;

  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  #clamp(n) {
    return n > 10 ? 10 : n;
  }

  increment(amount) {
    this.#count = this.#clamp(this.#count + amount);
  }

  reset() {
    this.#count = 0;
  }

  getCount() {
    return this.#count;
  }
}

const counter = new Counter("clicks");
counter.increment(4);
console.log(`After +4: ${counter.getCount()}`);
counter.increment(5);
console.log(`After +5: ${counter.getCount()}`);
counter.increment(3);
console.log(`After +3: ${counter.getCount()}`);
counter.reset();
console.log(`After reset: ${counter.getCount()}`);
console.log(JSON.stringify(counter));

steps.body:
A **getter** is a method that is read like a property. A **setter** runs when you assign to that property. Together they let a private field look like a normal field, with no `getBalance()` method needed, while the class keeps control.

```javascript
class Box {
  #size = 1;

  get size() {
    return this.#size;
  }

  set size(value) {
    if (value > 0) {
      this.#size = value;
    }
  }

  get volume() {
    return this.#size ** 3;
  }
}

const box = new Box();
box.size = 3;    // runs the setter
box.size = -5;   // ignored by the setter
box.volume;      // 27, no parentheses
```

Use a setter to clamp or ignore bad values instead of storing them. `volume` is a **computed property**: its value is worked out from private fields on every read, and with no setter it is read-only.

Gotchas:

- A setter with no getter reads as `undefined`, so define them in pairs.
- Assigning to a getter-only property, such as `box.volume = 8`, is silently ignored at the top level of this script. Inside class code it throws a `TypeError`, because class bodies run in strict mode.
- Inside the class, call your own setter with `this.size = ...` so its checks still run.
- Inside `get size()`, read `this.#size`, never `this.size`, which calls the getter again until the stack overflows.

example.code:
class Temperature {
  #celsius = 0;

  get celsius() {
    return this.#celsius;
  }

  set celsius(c) {
    this.#celsius = c < -273.15 ? -273.15 : c;
  }

  get fahrenheit() {
    return this.#celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;
  }

  set fahrenheit(f) {
    this.celsius = (f - 32) * 5 / 9;
  }

  reading() {
    return `${this.celsius.toFixed(2)} C / ${this.fahrenheit.toFixed(2)} F`;
  }
}

const temp = new Temperature();
temp.celsius = 25;
console.log(temp.reading());
temp.fahrenheit = 212;
console.log(temp.reading());
temp.celsius = -300;
console.log(temp.reading());
temp.fahrenheit = 50;
console.log(temp.reading());

