class Rectangle {
  #width;
  #height;

  constructor(width, height) {
    this.#width = width;
    this.#height = height;
  }

  get width() {
    return this.#width;
  }

  set width(w) {
    if (w >= 0) {
      this.#width = w;
    }
  }

  get area() {
    return this.#width * this.#height;
  }

  get perimeter() {
    return 2 * (this.#width + this.#height);
  }
}

const rect = new Rectangle(4, 3);
const show = () => {
  console.log(`width ${rect.width}, area ${rect.area}, perimeter ${rect.perimeter}`);
};
show();
rect.width = 10;
show();
rect.width = -5;
show();
rect.width = 7;
show();

steps.body:
A `static` member belongs to the class itself, not to each instance. You call a static method on the class name, much like `Math.max()`.

```javascript
class Temperature {
  static instances = 0;

  constructor(celsius) {
    this.celsius = celsius;
    Temperature.instances++;
  }

  static fromFahrenheit(f) {
    return new Temperature((f - 32) * 5 / 9);
  }
}

const boiling = Temperature.fromFahrenheit(212);
boiling.celsius;          // 100
Temperature.instances;    // 1
```

Two common uses:

- **Factory methods** such as `Temperature.fromFahrenheit(212)` build an instance from a different kind of input. They read better than a constructor with a flag.
- **Helpers** that work on several instances at once, such as `Point.distance(a, b)`.

A **static field** like `static count = 0` is one value shared by every instance. Update it from the constructor with `ClassName.count++`, so every `new` counts, including the ones a factory makes.

The gotcha: statics are not on instances. `boiling.instances` is `undefined`, and `boiling.fromFahrenheit` is not a function. Always go through the class name.

example.code:
class User {
  static count = 0;

  constructor(name, role) {
    this.name = name;
    this.role = role;
    User.count++;
  }

  static guest() {
    return new User("Guest", "visitor");
  }

  describe() {
    return `${this.name} (${this.role})`;
  }
}

const ada = new User("Ada", "admin");
const lin = new User("Lin", "editor");
const guest = User.guest();
console.log([ada, lin, guest].map((user) => user.describe()).join(", "));
console.log(`Users created: ${User.count}`);
console.log(`ada.count: ${ada.count}`);

