class Point {
  static created = 0;

  constructor(x, y) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
    Point.created++;
  }

  static origin() {
    return new Point(0, 0);
  }

  static distance(a, b) {
    return Math.hypot(b.x - a.x, b.y - a.y);
  }

  toString() {
    return `(${this.x}, ${this.y})`;
  }
}

const start = Point.origin();
const target = new Point(6, 8);
const corner = new Point(3, 4);
console.log(`From ${start} to ${target}: ${Point.distance(start, target)}`);
console.log(`From ${start} to ${corner}: ${Point.distance(start, corner)}`);
console.log(`Points created: ${Point.created}`);

steps.body:
`this` is not fixed when you write a method. It is set by how the method is **called**: in `cart.describe()`, `this` is whatever sits before the dot.

That has two consequences.

**Arrow callbacks work.** An arrow function has no `this` of its own, so inside a method it uses the method's `this`. That is why `this.items.forEach((item) => { this.total += item.price; })` updates the right object.

**Detached methods break.** `const f = cart.describe;` copies the function without the object. Calling `f()` has nothing before the dot, so `this` is `undefined` (class code is strict) and reading `this.owner` throws a `TypeError`. The same happens when you pass `cart.describe` as a callback. Two fixes:

```javascript harness
class Cart {
  constructor(owner, items) {
    this.owner = owner;
    this.items = items;
  }

  describe() {
    return `${this.owner}'s cart has ${this.items.length} items`;
  }
}

const cart = new Cart("Ada", [
  { name: "pen", price: 3 },
  { name: "book", price: 12 },
  { name: "mug", price: 8 },
]);
{{code}}
console.log(viaArrow());
console.log(viaBind());
```

```javascript
const viaArrow = () => cart.describe();
const viaBind = cart.describe.bind(cart);
```

`bind(obj)` returns a new function whose `this` is always `obj`.

A third option is a **class field that holds an arrow**. The arrow is created per instance while `this` is that instance, so it keeps it even when stored in a variable:

```javascript
summary = () => `${this.owner} pays ${this.total}`;
```

example.code:
class Cart {
  total = 0;
  summary = () => `${this.owner} pays ${this.total}`;

  constructor(owner, items) {
    this.owner = owner;
    this.items = items;
  }

  addUp() {
    this.total = 0;
    this.items.forEach((item) => {
      this.total += item.price;
    });
  }

  describe() {
    return `${this.owner}'s cart has ${this.items.length} items`;
  }
}

const cart = new Cart("Ada", [
  { name: "pen", price: 3 },
  { name: "book", price: 12 },
  { name: "mug", price: 8 },
]);
cart.addUp();
const describe = cart.describe.bind(cart);
console.log(describe());
const summary = cart.summary;
console.log(summary());

